WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:01.659
Okay.

00:00:01.659 --> 00:00:04.780
I did a story last week that was shocking.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.440
Uh, in terms of the reaction because of how
universally positive the reaction was from

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.030
95% of the audience.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:20.320
I can say almost anything on the show and
30, 40, if not 50% of the audience will vehemently

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.119
disagree with me to the point that they send
me insults and troll me and say horrible antisemitic

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:27.119
things.

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:30.500
But last year I said, hey, you know what I'm
thinking about getting off of social media.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.640
Uh, it's totally toxic.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.300
I've got people with five followers filling
my Twitter feed with nonsense attacks.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.300
I don't care about there's fake drama.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.930
There's the antisemitism, there's a very high
noise to signal ratio and it's interrupting

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:47.930
my work day.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.390
Also, it's in her interrupting my personal
life.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.250
It's preventing me from doing deeper work.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:59.050
It's basically become a nuisance and study
after study suggests it's not very healthy

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:04.760
either psychologically, and it was one of
those random, not super political in the partisan

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.500
sense clips that I often throw into the show.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.270
It has about a hundred thousand views.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:18.120
It has 96% likes, uh, and reaction to the
clip has exploded in an almost completely

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.340
positive way, which is so different than just
about everything we do on the show.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.820
Clearly this struck a nerve and I receive
more emails.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:31.150
People actually taking the time to email me
rather than just leave a comment online about

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.150
this story.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:37.470
Uh, that, that, that, that I've seen happen
with any other story that I can remember.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.820
Hundreds of emails and just the first couple
of days from people mostly saying, Hey David,

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.420
you're, you're right.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.320
Left wingers saying there's very little to
gain from spending more than like the minimum

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.630
required time for your job on social media
right wingers even saying, yeah, there's very

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.290
little advantage to spending all that time
on social media.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.600
And the most common suggestion that I received
was, you know, do whatever I want with social

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.670
media for my personal life up.

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:09.479
But there's no denying that for work purposes,
abandoning social media wouldn't be great

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.380
for the show since it's been such a help and
boost to the show.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:17.180
So the suggestion was David, just like make
it a very clearly defined piece of your job.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.640
Set a specific amount of time for checking
into social media and responding to people,

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.210
which is basically what I've been doing.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:30.610
But I decided over the last week to become
very, very narrow in terms of the social media

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.010
stuff and I actually find that it's making
me more responsive to the things I should

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:38.700
be responding to because I'm not having any
kind of overload.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.220
I'm checking in on my accounts in a really
limited way.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.540
If I see trolling, if I see nonsense stuff
that's not of obvious value, I ignore it.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.970
I mute the people.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.210
That's that.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:57.210
And one of the really big changes I made personally
is I didn't even remove the apps from my phone.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.980
I just removed them from my home screen.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.570
So when I go to my homescreen now, uh, I have
very little other than pocket, which is an

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:09.930
app I use to put articles that I find interesting
there.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:15.350
And just removing it from my home screen,
you might say, well you open your phone because

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:19.180
you got a text message and then you decide
I'm going to look at Facebook.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.270
It's just as easy to find it from your list
of Apps, even if it's not on the home screen.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:28.819
But what removing it from the homescreen did
is now I have to say, Oh, do I actually want

00:03:28.819 --> 00:03:33.890
to go and search out the Facebook app or the
Twitter APP or whatever.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:40.090
And just that little moment of pause almost
always leads to me saying, no, I actually

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.090
don't.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:47.220
And, uh, it has reduced my time on social
media by probably 90%, no intrusions into

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:48.620
what I'm doing.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.410
And you know what, yeah, some people will
send me a message and I'm not getting back

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.680
to them for three days, like over the weekend.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.390
My life is fine, everything's fine.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:04:00.980
And as far as the work piece goes, I'm checking
every social network in the morning, once

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.440
I'm done preparing the day's show.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.610
And then I'm checking it sometime between
three and five in the afternoon, sort of as

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:13.040
I'm, uh, working, uh, finished working on
the next day's show and that's it.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.300
And it's working really well.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:15.670
So, so far, so good.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:19.700
I look forward to talking to cal Newport about
this next week on the show.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:25.650
He's written some really good books about
digital minimalism and the distraction that

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:31.910
is sort of ingrained into us, um, by the social
social networks.

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:33.570
And I encourage you to do the same.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:38.060
And if you have questions for cal Newport
about this, either about his book, digital

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:44.740
minimum, minimum minimalism, digital minimalism,
or his book deep work, send them in to me

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.930
by email.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:46.930
Why not?

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:47.550
And I will talk to cal about them next week.

